Natural Language Processing (NLP) is a field of Artificial Intelligence (AI) that enables computers to understand, interpret, generate, and interact using human language (text or speech).

It combines:

Linguistics (how language works)

Computer Science (algorithms & programming)

Machine Learning & Deep Learning (learning from data)

Core Goals of NLP

Understand human language

Generate human-like language

Extract meaning from text or speech

Enable human–computer interaction

Main Components of NLP
1. Natural Language Understanding (NLU)

Focuses on comprehension.

Sentiment analysis

Entity recognition

Intent detection

Text classification

2. Natural Language Generation (NLG)

Focuses on producing language.

Text generation

Chatbots

Automatic summarization

Machine translation

Key NLP Tasks

Tokenization – Breaking text into words/sentences

Part-of-Speech Tagging – Identifying nouns, verbs, etc.

Named Entity Recognition (NER) – Detecting names, places, dates

Sentiment Analysis – Detecting emotions or opinions

Machine Translation – Translating between languages

Speech Recognition – Converting speech to text

Text Summarization – Creating shorter versions of text

Question Answering – Answering questions from text

NLP Techniques
1. Rule-Based Systems

Based on grammar rules

Early NLP systems

2. Statistical Methods

Probabilistic models

N-grams

Hidden Markov Models

3. Machine Learning

Naive Bayes

Support Vector Machines

4. Deep Learning (Modern NLP)

Neural Networks

RNN, LSTM

Transformers (e.g., OpenAI models)

Real-World Applications

Chatbots (like ChatGPT)

Voice assistants (Siri, Alexa)

Google Translate

Spam detection

Search engines

Autocomplete systems

Email filtering

Benefits of NLP

Automates language tasks

Improves customer service

Saves time

Extracts insights from large text data

Challenges in NLP

Ambiguity in language

Sarcasm and idioms

Multiple languages

Context understanding

Bias in models
